{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794357079,"nonce":"kd3urYvGsoCfTUdbCjPSkadG+7U=","message":"**Follow-up, correcting the earlier comment: moving the acquire off the event-loop thread does not fix it.**\n\nI tried the obvious change: `get_current_texture()` on a worker thread, the loop waits 2 ms for the image and otherwise skips the frame until the worker wakes it (release build, same reproducer, 20 runs each, two at a time):\n\n| build | runs with keys bundled | runs with a key-to-commit gap \u003e 500 ms |\n|---|---|---|\n| form, fs-blinc-2 | 18 | 12 |\n| form, with the worker | 0 | 18 (median key-to-commit 471 ms) |\n| Maps, fs-blinc-2 | 11 | 9 |\n| Maps, with the worker | 14 | 6 |\n\nSo the loop no longer stops, but the picture is not earlier: the compositor still answers once per second. In Maps the keys arrive bundled while the main thread sits in `ep_poll`, so there they are delivered late, not read late; the blocked acquire is only one of the symptoms.\n\nThe Wayland trace of a stalled stretch shows the cause on the compositor side: three commits answered by frame callbacks after about 11 ms, then 1.0 s of silence, then twelve queued callbacks and a buffer release within 3 ms. Every long acquire is such a silence. It looks like the virtual KWin (also KWin nested in a running compositor) repainting about once a second when nothing is damaged. Present mode (fifo, mailbox, immediate) and `desired_maximum_frame_latency` 1 to 3 do not remove it (3 runs each, only fifo with latency 3 had two clean runs); the pre_present_notify and callback pacing asked for is already in the code. Whether a real DRM session shows the same is not measured.\n\nI am not proposing a patch. The worker experiment is a small change (an `Arc\u003cSurface\u003e` and one thread), available if the maintainer wants it for robustness, but it does not shorten the wait.","files":null}]}